    "case_summary": "A man in his 60s presented with crusting, irritation, and recurrent sinus infections for the past 5 years. He denied hemoptysis, dyspnea, fatigue, or weight loss. No prior sinus surgeries or history of nasal trauma were reported. He mentioned occasional use of nasal irrigation with tap water.\n\nNasal endoscopy revealed destruction of the septal cartilage and turbinates, along with purulent, yellow drainage and crusting.\n\n- Laboratory tests: C-reactive protein, erythrocyte sedimentation rate, angiotensin-converting enzyme, white blood cell count, and antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody panel results were normal. HIV test was negative.- Imaging studies:  Title: Computed Tomography on Initial Presentation  Description: Axial and coronal scans reveal a substantial septal deformity with near complete destruction of the sinus septate. Shows thinning of the posterior wall of the frontal sinuses, erosion of the bone near the right lacrimal system.- Chest CT:  Description: Showed enlarged lymph nodes in the mediastinum.- Pathology/Microbiology:  - Sinus mucosa showed noncaseating granulomas  - Initial AFB staining was negative  - Culture showed growth of Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas  - AFB PCR probe of hilar lymph node was positive  - Second FESS AFB PCR probe was positive",
